1
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:15,020
On Tuesday, 16 August 1977,
Elvis Presley died in Memphis,

2
00:00:15,160 --> 00:00:17,015
Tennessee, at the age of forty-two.

3
00:00:24,767 --> 00:00:28,067
In the years since, one man
has carried much of the blame:

4
00:00:28,387 --> 00:00:32,627
his personal physician since
1967, Dr George Nichopoulos,

5
00:00:33,087 --> 00:00:34,107
known as Dr Nick.

6
00:00:34,702 --> 00:00:36,167
This film sticks to the record:

7
00:00:36,527 --> 00:00:38,607
the medical examiner,
the state medical board,

8
00:00:38,887 --> 00:00:40,747
the criminal court, and
the people who were there.

9
00:00:41,247 --> 00:00:44,027
Where the numbers disagree,
it says who gave each number,

10
00:00:44,507 --> 00:00:45,567
and it does not guess.

11
00:00:46,284 --> 00:00:49,424
In the summer of 1977
Elvis was still touring,

12
00:00:49,724 --> 00:00:53,324
and his last concert was
in Indianapolis on 26 June.

13
00:00:53,961 --> 00:00:58,486
In 1981 a Memphis judge ordered his estate
to sue his manager of more than twenty years,

14
00:00:58,766 --> 00:01:01,466
Colonel Tom Parker, which
is a story for another film.

15
00:01:02,049 --> 00:01:07,409
In July 1976 his father, Vernon
Presley, had fired three bodyguards,

16
00:01:07,809 --> 00:01:11,729
Red West, Sonny West and Dave
Hebler, and said it was to cut costs.

17
00:01:12,456 --> 00:01:16,116
The three men said they wrote a book
to make Elvis face up to his lifestyle,

18
00:01:16,396 --> 00:01:17,776
not for money or revenge.

19
00:01:18,530 --> 00:01:21,250
The writer Steve Dunleavy
worked with them, and the book,

20
00:01:21,570 --> 00:01:25,530
Elvis: What Happened?, reached
American shops in the summer of 1977,

21
00:01:26,190 --> 00:01:27,150
shortly before he died.

22
00:01:27,783 --> 00:01:32,043
Critics saw revenge, and the book
itself gives no sources for its claims.

23
00:01:32,770 --> 00:01:35,955
That same day NBC put the
three men on a special report.

24
00:02:04,667 --> 00:02:06,727
These were the accounts
of men who had been fired,

25
00:02:07,167 --> 00:02:11,887
and the book changed most of the names, which
is why some doubted individual incidents.

26
00:02:17,628 --> 00:02:22,153
That afternoon Elvis was found unresponsive
at Graceland and driven to Baptist Memorial

27
00:02:22,153 --> 00:02:24,893
Hospital in Memphis, where
he was pronounced dead.

28
00:02:25,502 --> 00:02:30,302
Dr Nichopoulos rode in the ambulance, and
afterwards he got written consent for an autopsy

29
00:02:30,302 --> 00:02:33,122
from Vernon Presley, the
Tennessee Supreme Court says.

30
00:02:33,772 --> 00:02:37,092
The autopsy was carried out at the
hospital, mainly by its own staff,

31
00:02:37,352 --> 00:02:39,812
with the county medical
examiner, Dr Jerry Francisco,

32
00:02:40,432 --> 00:02:41,652
taking part to some extent.

33
00:02:42,241 --> 00:02:47,141
That evening Francisco told reporters that
early findings pointed to a cardiac arrhythmia,

34
00:02:47,141 --> 00:02:49,521
and that he saw no sign of drug abuse.

35
00:02:50,196 --> 00:02:55,321
On 17 August President Jimmy Carter said
the death deprived the country of a part of

36
00:02:55,321 --> 00:02:58,601
itself, and called Elvis
unique and irreplaceable.

37
00:02:59,139 --> 00:03:01,564
Then came the laboratories,
and weeks of testing,

38
00:03:01,884 --> 00:03:03,164
and the readings did not agree.

39
00:03:03,720 --> 00:03:07,545
A university laboratory working
for Francisco found several drugs,

40
00:03:07,845 --> 00:03:11,105
including codeine, below
toxic or lethal levels.

41
00:03:11,764 --> 00:03:17,149
A California laboratory, Bio-Science, reported
codeine at about ten times a therapeutic

42
00:03:17,149 --> 00:03:20,649
dose, according to a 1991
account in Memphis magazine.

43
00:03:21,298 --> 00:03:24,738
The same account says a hospital
pathologist, Dr Eric Muirhead,

44
00:03:25,098 --> 00:03:28,478
told the family the findings were
consistent with a drug overdose.

45
00:03:29,202 --> 00:03:33,682
On 21 October 1977 Francisco
announced his ruling:

46
00:03:34,182 --> 00:03:37,802
hypertensive heart disease, with
coronary artery disease as a factor,

47
00:03:38,242 --> 00:03:39,782
and no part played by drugs.

48
00:03:40,507 --> 00:03:45,072
The death certificate, signed the day before,
listed a heart ailment and did not mention

49
00:03:45,072 --> 00:03:46,692
drugs, the same account says.

50
00:03:47,352 --> 00:03:50,652
Francisco held to that, saying
drugs played no role whatsoever.

51
00:03:51,324 --> 00:03:57,449
In 1982 two ABC journalists sued for
the autopsy and toxicology reports,

52
00:03:57,889 --> 00:04:02,669
and the Tennessee Supreme Court ruled that
the medical examiner need not produce them,

53
00:04:03,009 --> 00:04:05,949
because the autopsy was a
private project of the family.

54
00:04:06,598 --> 00:04:11,263
In 1994 the Tennessee health department
reviewed the records and found no evidence that

55
00:04:11,263 --> 00:04:15,523
Francisco had lied. It did not say whether
heart disease was the best diagnosis.

56
00:04:21,367 --> 00:04:24,667
George Nichopoulos began
treating Elvis in 1967,

57
00:04:25,207 --> 00:04:28,047
and from 1970 it was his full time job.

58
00:04:28,634 --> 00:04:33,354
At the 1981 trial the prosecutor said
Elvis's problem was drug abuse and addiction,

59
00:04:33,434 --> 00:04:35,954
and the defence called him
a terribly sick person.

60
00:04:36,600 --> 00:04:41,385
In 1973 and 1975 Elvis was
in hospital three times,

61
00:04:41,385 --> 00:04:44,925
and at the trial a state
witness, Dr Alvin Cummins,

62
00:04:45,325 --> 00:04:46,225
said he had been addicted.

63
00:04:46,845 --> 00:04:49,690
Prosecutors counted the prescriptions
written in Elvis's name:

64
00:04:49,690 --> 00:04:55,590
more than four thousand doses in 1975, and
nearly fifteen thousand more between the start

65
00:04:55,590 --> 00:04:57,470
of 1976 and his death.

66
00:04:57,968 --> 00:05:02,398
Prosecutors put that at about nineteen
thousand doses in thirty months,

67
00:05:02,658 --> 00:05:06,758
and Cummins called it an outrageous
and dangerous overuse of drugs.

68
00:05:07,476 --> 00:05:09,721
Other accounts, with no
court record behind them,

69
00:05:09,961 --> 00:05:13,141
say more than ten thousand
doses in 1977 alone.

70
00:05:13,788 --> 00:05:17,868
The defence asked Cummins about amphetamines
mailed to Elvis from the West Coast,

71
00:05:18,348 --> 00:05:19,748
and he said he knew nothing of that.

72
00:05:20,240 --> 00:05:23,780
His lawyer, James Neal, said Nichopoulos
had tried to get Elvis to stop,

73
00:05:24,400 --> 00:05:27,700
had consulted other doctors, and had
asked friends and family for help.

74
00:05:28,283 --> 00:05:33,023
Years later, Nichopoulos told a Las Vegas
television station that many prescriptions

75
00:05:33,023 --> 00:05:35,223
in Elvis's name were not for Elvis.

76
00:05:50,795 --> 00:05:54,900
In 2009 he said he did not regret
any of the medications he gave Elvis,

77
00:05:55,280 --> 00:05:56,260
and called them necessities.

78
00:06:02,133 --> 00:06:08,453
The scrutiny began in September 1979, when
ABC's programme 20/20 accused officials of

79
00:06:08,453 --> 00:06:13,393
covering up a drug death, and a tip led state
investigators to Elvis's prescription records.

80
00:06:14,186 --> 00:06:19,966
In January 1980 the state medical board found
Nichopoulos guilty of ten counts of overprescribing,

81
00:06:20,426 --> 00:06:24,186
one involving Elvis, and suspended
his licence for three months,

82
00:06:24,286 --> 00:06:25,586
with three years of probation.

83
00:06:26,336 --> 00:06:32,256
He was also charged in Memphis, and his
criminal trial began in October 1981 before a

84
00:06:32,256 --> 00:06:34,356
jury of six men and six women.

85
00:06:34,982 --> 00:06:37,802
Prosecutors said the charges
were about overprescribing,

86
00:06:38,362 --> 00:06:39,602
not about the cause of death.

87
00:06:40,111 --> 00:06:43,591
In early November 1981 the jury
acquitted him on every count.

88
00:06:59,062 --> 00:07:01,407
In the court and board
records found for this film,

89
00:07:01,967 --> 00:07:04,727
no one is held to have caused
the death of Elvis Presley.

90
00:07:05,230 --> 00:07:11,990
In March 1992 new charges were filed, and
on 19 July 1995 the board found him guilty

91
00:07:11,990 --> 00:07:16,390
of gross malpractice and unethical
conduct involving thirteen patients,

92
00:07:16,930 --> 00:07:19,990
including Jerry Lee Lewis,
and revoked his licence.

93
00:07:20,747 --> 00:07:25,027
The board refused to give the licence
back in 1998 and again in 2000,

94
00:07:25,427 --> 00:07:29,827
and Nichopoulos died in
Memphis on 24 February 2016,

95
00:07:30,087 --> 00:07:30,931
aged eighty-eight.

96
00:07:31,376 --> 00:07:36,221
And the autopsy? Online articles say it was
sealed for fifty years and will be opened

97
00:07:36,221 --> 00:07:38,301
on 16 August 2027.

98
00:07:38,931 --> 00:07:42,911
The Tennessee Supreme Court called that
autopsy a private project of the family,

99
00:07:43,411 --> 00:07:48,251
and the research for this film found no
law or court order setting 2027 for it.

100
00:07:48,775 --> 00:07:53,595
Tennessee law does say that death records held
by the state registrar become public fifty

101
00:07:53,595 --> 00:07:56,935
years after death, and that
may be what the date refers to.

102
00:07:57,481 --> 00:08:00,506
The record fixes a death
certificate that says heart disease,

103
00:08:01,246 --> 00:08:04,446
three laboratory readings
that disagree, an acquittal,

104
00:08:05,046 --> 00:08:07,086
and a licence lost over other patients.

105
00:08:07,986 --> 00:08:11,666
It does not settle what the drugs
did, and this film will not guess.

106
00:08:12,367 --> 00:08:16,747
The record gives a certificate, a
verdict and numbers nobody agrees on.

107
00:08:17,527 --> 00:08:19,707
When you think of the end
of Elvis Presley's life,

108
00:08:20,047 --> 00:08:23,267
who do you ask first: the
doctor, or the people around him?
